  "text": "Allophone: A variant form of a phoneme that does not contrast with other variants in meaning.\n\nAccent: A stress pattern or a distinctive way of pronouncing a language, often associated with regional or social variation.\n\nAffixation: The process of adding affixes (prefixes, suffixes, infixes, etc.) to a word to modify its meaning or grammatical function.\n\nAllograph: A variant form of a grapheme that does not change the meaning of a word, such as different spellings of the same letter.\n\nAllophony: The occurrence of different allophones of a phoneme in different phonetic environments.\n\nArticulatory Phonetics: The study of how speech sounds are produced by the vocal tract.\n\nAssimilation: A phonological process where one sound becomes more like a neighboring sound.\n\nBorrowing: The process of adopting words from another language into one's own language.\n\nComplementizer: A word or phrase that introduces a clause, such as \"that\" or \"whether.\"\n\nCompound: A word formed by joining two or more words, such as \"notebook\" or \"blackboard.\"\n\nConsonant: A speech sound produced by obstructing the airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant Cluster: A group of two or more consonants that appear together in a word without a vowel between them.\n\nConsonantization: The process by which a vowel becomes a consonant due to phonological influences.\n\nConsonant Manner of Articulation: The way in which the airstream is modified during the production of a consonant, such as stops, fricatives, etc.\n\nConsonant Place of Articulation: The location in the vocal tract where a consonant is produced, such as bilabial, alveolar, etc.\n\nContour: The distinctive shape or pattern of a tone or intonation contour in speech.\n\nConstituent: A group of words that functions as a single unit in a sentence.\n\nContrast: The ability of two sounds to distinguish meaning, indicating that they are different phonemes.\n\nCohesion: The use of grammatical and lexical devices to link parts of a discourse together.\n\nCohort: A group of words that share a common prefix, suffix, or root.\n\nCohort Effect: A phenomenon in language acquisition where words that are similar in form are learned together.\n\nCommunication: The exchange of information between individuals through the use of symbols or signs.\n\nComprehensibility: The extent to which a spoken or written message can be understood by the listener or reader.\n\nConlang: A constructed language, created for a specific purpose such as a fictional work.\n\nConnotation: The emotional or cultural associations a word may carry.\n\nConsonant: A speech sound produced by obstructing the airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant Invention: The creation of a new consonant sound in a language, often due to phonological or phonetic changes.\n\nConsonantization: The process by which a vowel becomes a consonant due to phonological influences.\n\nConsonant Mutation: A change in the phonological features of a consonant, often for morphological or syntactic reasons.\n\nCorpus: A collection of texts or speech samples used for linguistic analysis.\n\nCrack: A very short, unstressed vowel sound in some languages, such as in Hawaiian.\n\nDeletion: The omission of a phoneme or morpheme in speech or writing.\n\nDevoicing: The process by which a voiced consonant becomes voiceless in a certain phonological environment.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that glides from one vowel quality to another within the same syllable.\n\nDiscreteness: The property of language where individual units (such as sounds or words) can be distinguished.\n\nDiscreteness: The",
